<BaseLayout title="Privacy — Biochemistrypedia" description="What Biochemistrypedia stores, where, who can see it, and how to download or delete it. No ads, nothing sold.">
  <article class="wrap tc">
    <p class="tc-eyebrow">Privacy</p>
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="tc-lede">This site is free and has no ads. We don't sell anything about you, and we don't share it. Here is everything we keep.</p>

    <h2>Without an account</h2>
    <p>You can use every lesson without an account. Your progress, highlights and notes are saved in your own browser, on your own device. They are not sent to us. If you clear your browser's site data, they are gone.</p>
    <p>One exception: if you joined a teacher's class, what you do in the lessons your teacher assigned goes to your teacher. That is part of the class, not an account. See <a href="/teach/privacy/">classes privacy</a>.</p>

    <h2>With a learner account</h2>
    <p>A learner account is optional. It keeps your highlights, notes and progress the same on all your devices, and turns on the study helpers described below. Learner accounts are for people 13 and older. (Teachers who run a class have a separate teacher account, described on the <a href="/teach/privacy/">classes privacy page</a>.)</p>
    <p>If you make one, we store:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A name</strong> to show on your account. With a passkey you choose it. With Google it is your first name from Google.</li>
      <li><strong>Your email address</strong>, only if you sign in with Google. We use it for nothing except showing you which account you're in. We send no email.</li>
      <li><strong>Google's account number for you</strong>, if you use Google, so we know it's you next time. We never see your Google password.</li>
      <li><strong>Your passkeys' public keys</strong>, if you use passkeys. The private part never leaves your device.</li>
      <li><strong>Which devices are signed in</strong>, as a one-way scrambled code and a date.</li>
      <li><strong>Your highlights, notes and progress</strong>: the passages you highlighted, your notes, which lessons you opened or finished, and how you did on quiz questions.</li>
      <li><strong>When you made the account</strong> and when you last used it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Study helpers (AI, learner accounts only)</h2>
    <p>If you are signed in, a lesson can offer three small helpers: <strong>Explain another way</strong> (select a passage), <strong>Get feedback on my answer</strong> (in a claim, evidence and reasoning box) and <strong>Make flashcards from my highlights</strong>. They use an AI model, Claude, made by Anthropic, through the site owner's own account. You never need an account with Anthropic, and the site pays.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>What is sent:</strong> the lesson's own text, plus only what that helper needs: the passage you selected and your optional question; your written answer; or the highlights and notes on that lesson. Nothing that names you is sent: no name, email or account number.</li>
      <li><strong>What is kept:</strong> nothing you selected, wrote or highlighted. It is sent once to make the reply, and the site does not store it or write it to any log. Anthropic handles it under its commercial terms, which do not allow it to train models on it. The site keeps only counts and cost, to stay inside a spending limit.</li>
      <li><strong>Cards you keep</strong> are saved in your own browser, like your notebook. They are not synced to your account yet.</li>
      <li><strong>Every reply is labelled as written by an AI model</strong> and is built only from that lesson's text, but it can still be wrong. The lesson is the source to trust.</li>
      <li>The helpers are not offered on Biology Together or grade 7 Life Science, or to a student who joined a teacher's class on that device.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="planner">Study plans</h2>
    <p>With a learner account you can ask the study planner for a week-by-week plan. It uses an AI model from Anthropic, through the site owner's own Anthropic account, to choose lessons, films, simulations and flashcards from this site.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>What is sent:</strong> what you type in the planner's form (your goal, how many weeks and hours, your level and what you already know), sent once to make the plan. When you press <strong>Re-plan from here</strong>, the plan's saved goal and the list of lessons you have marked done are sent. Your name, email and account are never sent.</li>
      <li><strong>What is kept:</strong> nothing, unless you save the plan. A saved plan keeps the lessons and films in it, the name and notes you give it, the goal you typed (so it can be re-planned), and when you saved it. It is part of your account: in <strong>Download my data</strong>, and deleted with the plan or the account.</li>
      <li><strong>What is not kept:</strong> we don't store or log what you type to make a plan. We count only how many plans are made and what they cost.</li>
      <li>Anthropic's commercial terms apply to what it receives; under them it does not train its models on it.</li>
      <li>Students in teacher classes are never sent to the planner. Where you stand in a plan is worked out in your own browser.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where it is kept</h2>
    <p>On Cloudflare, which hosts this site, in its database service. Sign-in with Google goes through Google; Google's own privacy policy covers what Google does.</p>

    <h2>Who can see it</h2>
    <p>Only you. Teachers can't see your learner account, email, notes or synced progress, and nor can anyone else on the site. If you are also in a class, your teacher sees your class answers and nickname, as with any student in the class, but nothing links them to your learner account. The site's owner could look at the database to fix a problem, but does not read learners' notes.</p>

    <h2>Download or delete it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>On <a href="/account/">your account page</a>, <strong>Download my data</strong> gives you a file with everything above.</li>
      <li><strong>Delete my account</strong> deletes your account and everything synced to it, at once. It can't be undone. The copy in your own browser stays until you clear it.</li>
      <li>Signing out can also remove your notes and progress from that device, for a shared computer.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Other things we count</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>We count page visits with Plausible. It sets no cookies and collects nothing that identifies you.</li>
      <li>To stop people hammering sign-in, we count attempts per connection using a one-way scrambled form of the IP address. Those counts are deleted within a day.</li>
      <li>Cookies: one keeps you signed in, one tells the page you are signed in, and one lasts ten minutes during sign-in. No tracking or advertising cookies.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Classes</h2>
    <p>Teacher classes work differently. A student joins with a nickname and a student code, and never needs a learner account. A student 13 or older can also make a learner account if they want sync, but the two stay separate: the teacher sees class answers only, and the account stays private to the student. Students under 13 should not make a learner account. See <a href="/teach/privacy/">classes privacy</a>.</p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p><a href="/contact">Get in touch</a>.</p>
    <p class="tc-muted">First edition, November 2026; updated November 14, 2026 for study plans. This page changes if the features change.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
